<DocsLayout title="CLI Reference">
  <h1>CLI Reference</h1>
  <p>
    The primary way to use claustre is the TUI workbench — just run
    <code>claustre</code>. The CLI commands below are an alternative for
    scripting or when you prefer the terminal.
  </p>

  <h2>Workbench</h2>
  <pre><code>claustre                  # Launch TUI (default command)
claustre dashboard        # Same as above, explicit</code></pre>
  <p>
    The default command opens the terminal workbench with a sidebar, main
    buffer, right-hand inspector, and bottom status rows. From there you can
    manage projects, create and launch tasks, inspect GitHub-backed threads and
    reviews, and interact with embedded Claude Code terminals.
  </p>

  <h2>Project Management</h2>
  <pre><code>claustre add-project &lt;name&gt; [path]         # Register a project (path defaults to ".")
claustre remove-project &lt;name&gt;             # Remove a project and its data
claustre list-projects                     # List all projects with session/task counts</code></pre>

  <p>
    <strong>add-project</strong> — Registers a git repository as a claustre
    project. The path defaults to the current directory if omitted.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>remove-project</strong> — Removes a project and all its associated
    tasks and sessions from the database.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>list-projects</strong> — Shows all registered projects with their
    task and session counts.
  </p>

  <h2>Task Management</h2>
  <pre><code>claustre add-task &lt;project&gt; &lt;title&gt; [-d description] [-m mode]
claustre list-tasks &lt;project&gt;
claustre export &lt;project&gt; [-o path]</code></pre>

  <p>
    <strong>add-task</strong> — Creates a new task for a project. Mode can be
    <code>supervised</code> (default, one task at a time with user interaction),
    <code>autonomous</code> (auto-chains the next task from the queue when
    done), or <code>exploration</code> (open-ended research). The CLI only
    supports setting mode and description; other parameters (base, branch, push
    mode, review loop, subtasks) require the
    <a href="/tasks#creating-a-task">TUI task form</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>list-tasks</strong> — Lists all tasks for a project with status
    symbols: <code>&#x29D6;</code> pending, <code>&#x25CF;</code> working,
    <code>&#x25D0;</code> in_review, <code>&#x2713;</code> done,
    <code>&#x2717;</code> error.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>export</strong> — Exports all tasks and project stats to a JSON file.
    Defaults to <code>&lt;project&gt;-export.json</code> in the current
    directory.
  </p>

  <h2>Statistics</h2>
  <pre><code>claustre stats &lt;project&gt;</code></pre>

  <p>
    Shows aggregate statistics for a project: total tasks completed, total
    sessions, time spent, tokens used, and cost.
  </p>

  <h2>Skills (skills.sh)</h2>
  <pre><code>claustre skills                            # List installed global skills
claustre skills find &lt;query&gt;               # Search the skills.sh registry
claustre skills add &lt;package&gt; [-p project] # Install a skill (globally or per-project)
claustre skills remove &lt;name&gt; [-p project] # Remove a skill
claustre skills update                     # Update all installed skills</code></pre>

  <p>
    Skills are managed via
    <a href="https://skills.sh" target="_blank" rel="noopener">skills.sh</a>, a
    package registry for Claude Code skills. They extend Claude's capabilities
    with domain-specific knowledge and tools.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>skills</strong> (no subcommand) — Lists all globally installed
    skills.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>skills find</strong> — Searches the skills.sh registry for packages
    matching the query.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>skills add</strong> — Installs a skill package. By default, skills
    are installed globally. Use <code>-p project</code> to install per-project.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>skills remove</strong> — Removes an installed skill. Use
    <code>-p project</code> to remove a per-project skill.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>skills update</strong> — Updates all installed skills to their latest
    versions.
  </p>
  <p>
    The TUI also has a skills panel (press <code>i</code>) for browsing and
    managing skills interactively.
  </p>

  <h2>Desktop App</h2>
  <pre><code>claustre app                           # Launch the native macOS desktop app</code></pre>
  <p>
    Opens the native macOS desktop application built with
    <a href="https://tauri.app" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tauri</a>.
    The desktop app provides the same project and task management features as
    the TUI in a windowed GUI. See the
    <a href="/desktop-app">Desktop App</a> page for details.
  </p>

  <h2>Setup &amp; Maintenance</h2>
  <pre><code>claustre configure                     # Onboarding wizard: check prerequisites, configure permissions
claustre health-check                  # Verify the binary is functional (used by auto-update)
claustre rollback                      # Revert to previous binary after a bad update</code></pre>

  <p>
    <strong>configure</strong> &mdash; An interactive onboarding wizard that:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Checks that prerequisites are installed (<code>git</code>, <code>claude</code>, <code>gh</code>)</li>
    <li>
      Compares your <code>~/.claude/settings.json</code> permissions against
      the recommended set (defined in <code>[permissions]</code> in
      <code>config.toml</code>) and offers to apply missing entries
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The TUI also runs a sanity check on startup &mdash; a warning banner
    appears in the title bar if permissions are misaligned, and pressing
    <code>c</code> opens the configure overlay.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>health-check</strong> &mdash; Prints a success message and exits.
    Used internally by the auto-update process to verify that a newly
    downloaded binary works before replacing the current one.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>rollback</strong> &mdash; Replaces the current claustre binary
    with the backup stored at <code>~/.claustre/bin/claustre.prev</code>.
    Use this if an auto-update introduced a broken build.
  </p>

  <h2>Sync</h2>
  <pre><code>claustre sync init [url]              # Initialize sync repo (clone if URL given)
claustre sync push                    # Export state, commit, push
claustre sync pull                    # Pull from remote, import into local DB
claustre sync cd                      # Change to sync directory (requires shell-init)
claustre shell-init                   # Print shell integration script</code></pre>

  <p>
    <strong>sync init</strong> — Sets up a git repo at
    <code>~/.claustre/sync/</code>. If a URL is provided, clones from it.
    Otherwise initializes a new local repo (add a remote later with
    <code>git -C ~/.claustre/sync remote add origin &lt;url&gt;</code>).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>sync push</strong> — Exports all projects, tasks, and subtasks as
    JSON files to the sync repo, commits, and pushes to the remote.
    Idempotent — does nothing if state hasn't changed.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>sync pull</strong> — Pulls from the remote and upserts tasks into
    the local database. Projects are matched by name. Unregistered projects
    are skipped with a message.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>sync cd</strong> — Changes to the sync directory
    (<code>~/.claustre/sync/</code>). Requires shell integration:
    add <code>eval "$(claustre shell-init)"</code> to your
    <code>.zshrc</code> or <code>.bashrc</code>.
    Without shell integration, prints the path to stdout.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>shell-init</strong> — Prints a shell function that wraps
    <code>claustre</code> to enable commands that need to affect the
    parent shell (like <code>sync cd</code>). Add
    <code>eval "$(claustre shell-init)"</code> to your shell rc file.
  </p>
  <p>
    See <a href="/sync">Sync Across Machines</a> for setup guides and
    detailed workflows.
  </p>

  <h2>GitHub Cache</h2>
  <pre><code>claustre github status                # Show GitHub App / gh auth and cache summary
claustre github login                 # Authenticate via GitHub App device flow (or gh fallback)
claustre github sync [--project name] # Hydrate issue / PR / review / Projects v2 caches
claustre github disconnect            # Clear GitHub App session (or gh fallback)</code></pre>

  <p>
    <strong>github login</strong> now follows the same setup-first flow as the TUI:
    it uses a bundled or custom GitHub App device flow when app metadata is
    available, and otherwise falls back to a browser-based <code>gh auth login</code>
    flow. On the GitHub CLI fallback path, Claustre requests
    <code>repo</code>, <code>read:org</code>, <code>read:project</code>, and
    <code>project</code> so Projects v2 board APIs are available. GitHub App sessions are stored under
    <code>~/.claustre/github-app-session.json</code>; GitHub CLI sessions stay in
    the normal <code>gh</code> credential store.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>github sync</strong> hydrates the GitHub cache tables for each
    registered project. Claustre now prefers GitHub App user tokens for REST
    calls and falls back to the local <code>gh</code> CLI when no GitHub App
    session is available. Issue bodies, PR bodies, review events, and inline
    review comments are stored in raw markdown, plain text, and rendered HTML
    forms when available. Claustre also discovers GitHub Projects v2 for the
    selected repo owner, caches the selected project's items, and uses that
    data to drive the Sprint Board.
  </p>

  <h2>Threads</h2>
  <pre><code>claustre thread launch --task-id &lt;id&gt; [--provider claude|codex|gemini|local] [--profile name] [--runtime-profile name] [--workflow name]
claustre thread focus --thread-id &lt;id&gt;
claustre thread switch-provider --thread-id &lt;id&gt; --provider claude|codex|gemini|local [--profile name]
claustre thread paste-clipboard --thread-id &lt;id&gt; [--draft-key key]</code></pre>

  <p>
    <strong>thread launch</strong> creates a GitHub-native thread workspace for
    an existing task while preserving compatibility with the current
    task/session engine. It creates a shadow session/worktree, records the
    thread metadata, optionally starts the selected runtime profile from
    <code>sandbox.yaml</code>, and can attach a named workflow immediately.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>thread paste-clipboard</strong> performs smart clipboard paste for a
    thread. If the clipboard contains text, it prints the text. If it contains
    an image, Claustre stores the image under
    <code>~/.claustre/attachments/&lt;thread-id&gt;/</code>, records a
    <code>thread_attachments</code> row, and prints the stored asset path.
  </p>

  <h2>Workflows</h2>
  <pre><code>claustre workflow list [--project name]
claustre workflow run --name &lt;workflow&gt; --thread-id &lt;id&gt;
claustre workflow resume --run-id &lt;id&gt;
claustre workflow approve --run-id &lt;id&gt; --stage &lt;stage&gt;
claustre workflow artifact --run-id &lt;id&gt;</code></pre>

  <p>
    Workflows are ordered multi-stage pipelines such as
    <code>plan_first_tdd</code>, <code>review_fix_loop</code>,
    <code>bug_triage_then_patch</code>, and
    <code>research_then_plan</code>. Definitions are loaded from built-ins,
    <code>~/.claustre/workflows/</code>, and repo-local
    <code>.claustre/workflows/</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Runtime</h2>
  <pre><code>claustre runtime up --thread-id &lt;id&gt; [--profile name]
claustre runtime down --thread-id &lt;id&gt;
claustre runtime restart --thread-id &lt;id&gt;
claustre runtime health --thread-id &lt;id&gt;
claustre runtime logs --thread-id &lt;id&gt; --service &lt;name&gt;</code></pre>

  <p>
    Runtime commands operate on the current thread worktree and are driven by
    <code>sandbox.yaml</code>. Claustre supports named build steps, command and
    docker-compose services, named checks, and runtime profiles. Service state
    is persisted locally in <code>thread_runtime_state</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Open Targets</h2>
  <pre><code>claustre open --thread &lt;id&gt;</code></pre>

  <p>
    <strong>open</strong> is a lightweight deep-link target for notifications
    and external automation. It prints the linked thread metadata today and is
    intended to become the hand-off point for focusing an existing thread in
    the TUI.
  </p>

  <h2>Internal Commands</h2>
  <p>
    claustre also has internal commands used by hooks and the autonomous task
    runner. These are not meant to be run manually.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>session-update</strong> — Called by the Stop and UserPromptSubmit
    hooks to sync session state back to the database. Updates Claude status,
    detects PRs, records token usage, and handles task status transitions.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>feed-next</strong> &mdash; The autonomous task chain runner. Runs as a
    blocking loop inside an embedded PTY: picks the next pending autonomous task,
    launches Claude as a subprocess, waits for completion, then loops to pick up
    the next task.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>session-host</strong> &mdash; A detached PTY owner that runs as a
    separate process and serves the PTY over a Unix socket. Survives TUI
    restarts &mdash; the TUI reconnects to existing session-hosts on startup.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>review-loop</strong> &mdash; Monitors an <code>in_review</code>
    task's PR for new comments. Polls at a configurable interval, launches
    Claude to evaluate and implement accepted feedback, then loops. Spawned
    automatically by the TUI when a task with the review loop flag transitions
    to <code>in_review</code>.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
